;
/* data.js */
const stages=[
['Research interest','Find the question behind your curiosity.','Choose a topic you care about, then narrow it to a specific group, setting, and issue. A manageable question is more useful than an enormous theme.','Which experience or observation makes you want to know more?','Write three interests. Pick one and name the people, place, and issue you want to understand.'],
['Research problem','Turn an observation into a researchable problem.','Describe what is happening, why it matters, and what remains unclear. Separate evidence of a problem from your assumptions about its cause.','What is the problem, who experiences it, and what evidence shows it matters?','Draft a paragraph with the context, problem, and consequence. Mark any claim that still needs evidence.'],
['Literature search','Find the conversation your research will join.','Break your topic into key concepts and synonyms. Combine concepts with AND and synonyms with OR. Record databases, search terms, and dates so your process is traceable.','What three concepts and alternative terms could help you find relevant studies?','Create a search string and a search log. Screen results using clear inclusion criteria.'],
['Literature analysis','Look for patterns across the evidence.','Read for the question, method, sample, findings, and limitations. Compare studies rather than summarizing one paper at a time. Use the literature matrix to keep evidence organized.','Where do the studies agree, disagree, or use different methods?','Compare three relevant papers in a matrix. Record the full citation and distinguish findings from your interpretation.'],
['Research gap','Identify what remains uncertain.','A useful gap is supported by the literature: an unresolved explanation, a conflicting finding, an underexamined context, or a methodological limitation. A topic being new to you does not establish a gap.','What is still unknown, and which studies demonstrate that uncertainty?','Write a gap statement supported by sources, then explain why addressing it would matter.'],
['Research questions','Ask something your study can answer.','Align your question with the problem and gap. Be specific about the phenomenon, population, and setting. Avoid causal wording unless your study design can support causal conclusions.','What focused question could you answer with realistically available evidence?','Draft one main question and up to three supporting questions. Check scope and feasibility.'],
['Objectives','Translate your question into a clear purpose.','Objectives describe what your study will accomplish. Use precise verbs such as describe, compare, explore, or estimate. Every objective should connect to a research question.','What will you describe, explore, or compare to answer your question?','Pair each research question with an objective and identify the evidence needed.'],
['Methodology','Choose a credible way to find out.','Select a design that fits your question. Explain participants or sources, sampling, data collection, analysis, limitations, and ethical safeguards. Confirm institutional requirements before collecting data.','What data do you need, how can you access it, and how will you analyze it?','Draft a methods plan. Include consent, privacy, feasibility, and any required ethics review.'],
['Research proposal','Connect your thinking into one coherent plan.','Bring your context, problem, literature, gap, questions, objectives, and methods together. Check that each part supports the next. Follow your institution’s format and citation requirements.','Can a reader follow the connection from your problem to your proposed method?','Use the proposal starter and readiness checklist. Ask a supervisor or peer to identify any missing links.']
];

const course = [
{phase:0,time:8,output:'A focused topic statement',title:'Find a topic worth exploring',subtitle:'Move from a broad interest to a question you can investigate.',
sections:[['Begin with an observation','Research often begins with something that puzzles you: a pattern you notice, an explanation that seems incomplete, or a practical difficulty people face. Write the observation before choosing a method or deciding what the answer should be.'],['Set useful boundaries','Name the phenomenon, the people or materials you will study, and the setting. Consider what you can access within your time and resources. You can adjust these boundaries as you learn more; a topic statement is a starting point, not a contract.'],['Test your starting point','Do an exploratory search. Notice the terms researchers use and whether there is enough relevant material to understand the topic. If your search returns nothing useful, try synonyms and speak with a librarian before assuming you have found a research gap.']],
before:'I want to research student wellbeing.',after:'I want to understand how first-year students at my institution describe balancing paid work with study.',example:'The second version identifies a group, a setting, and a specific experience. It leaves the answer open instead of assuming paid work is harmful.',trap:'Choosing a topic because you already know what you want to prove. Write down competing explanations and stay open to them.',
steps:['List three observations that interest you.','Choose one and name the group, setting, and phenomenon.','Check whether the scope fits your available time and access.'],prompt:'Write your topic statement. Name the phenomenon, population or materials, and setting. Explain why it interests you and what you can realistically access.',placeholder:'I want to understand…\nAmong / using…\nIn the context of…\nThis matters because…\nI can access…',question:'Which topic is most ready for an initial literature search?',answers:['The effects of everything on student success','How first-year working students describe managing study time at one institution','Proof that having a job causes academic failure'],correct:1,feedback:'A bounded, open question gives you useful search concepts without assuming the conclusion.',checks:['The topic names a specific phenomenon.','The setting or population is clear.','I have considered time and access.']},
{phase:0,time:10,output:'A defensible problem statement',title:'Define the problem clearly',subtitle:'Explain what needs to be understood, and why it matters.',
sections:[['Distinguish a topic from a problem','A topic names an area of interest. A research problem identifies a specific uncertainty or difficulty within it. “Working students” is a topic. Not understanding how a particular group manages competing demands could become a problem, if you can establish its relevance.'],['Build an evidence trail','Support contextual claims with credible sources. Separate documented evidence, personal observations, and assumptions. A memorable anecdote may inspire your question but cannot by itself establish how widespread a problem is.'],['Write a connected argument','Move from the setting to the specific issue, then to what remains unclear and why understanding it would be useful. Avoid exaggerating urgency or claiming that your study will solve a problem beyond its scope.']],
before:'Students work too much, so universities must change their policies.',after:'This study will examine how first-year students describe balancing work and study at Institution X. The problem statement must first establish the local context and show what current evidence does not explain.',example:'This is an illustrative scaffold, not a factual claim about an institution. Replace general statements with verified evidence before using it in a proposal.',trap:'Confusing your preferred solution with the problem. Understand the issue before promising an intervention.',steps:['Identify the issue and the people or context involved.','List the evidence needed to support each factual claim.','Explain the uncertainty and the value of addressing it.'],prompt:'Draft a problem statement with context, evidence, uncertainty, and significance. Use [source needed] where a claim still needs support.',placeholder:'Context and evidence…\nThe specific issue…\nWhat remains unclear…\nWhy understanding it matters…',question:'Which sentence most needs supporting evidence?',answers:['I am interested in students’ experiences.','Most first-year students at this institution struggle to balance work and study.','The proposed study asks about time-management experiences.'],correct:1,feedback:'“Most” makes a claim about prevalence. It needs suitable evidence about the relevant population.',checks:['Contextual claims have sources or are flagged.','The uncertainty is specific.','The significance does not overpromise.']},
{phase:0,time:12,output:'A repeatable search strategy',title:'Search with a strategy',subtitle:'Find relevant evidence and keep a record of how you found it.',
sections:[['Map your concepts','Break the topic into two or three concepts. List synonyms, spelling variants, and terms used in the literature. A population may be described differently across disciplines, so revise your vocabulary as you read.'],['Combine and refine','OR usually groups alternatives; AND combines concepts. Quotation marks often search a phrase, but syntax varies by database. Begin with a broad enough query, examine relevant results, and adjust one part at a time. Avoid so many filters that important work disappears.'],['Record the process','Keep the database, date, exact query, filters, and useful results in a search log. Note why you included or excluded sources. Follow references from useful papers and look for later work that cites them. An ordinary course review is not automatically a systematic review.']],
before:'Searching only “student problems” in a general search engine.',after:'("first-year students" OR freshmen) AND (employment OR "paid work") AND ("time management" OR "study demands")',example:'This is a starting query. Test it in a relevant library database, check its syntax, and change terms when the results reveal better language.',trap:'Treating the first page of results as the complete literature. Search more than one appropriate source and record the limits of your search.',steps:['Create a concept-and-synonym table.','Test a query in an appropriate database.','Log the query and explain your screening decisions.'],prompt:'Record your concepts, synonyms, planned database, draft query, and initial inclusion criteria.',placeholder:'Concepts and synonyms…\nDatabase…\nSearch query…\nInclude sources when…\nExclude sources when…\nSearch date and notes…',question:'What is OR usually used for in a database query?',answers:['To combine alternative terms for a concept','To require every search concept to appear','To guarantee that results are peer reviewed'],correct:0,feedback:'OR broadens a concept with alternatives. Database syntax and indexing still need to be checked.',checks:['Synonyms are included.','The exact query and database are recorded.','Screening criteria connect to my question.']},
{phase:1,time:12,output:'An evidence synthesis',title:'Read for patterns, not just summaries',subtitle:'Compare what studies tell you and how they reached their conclusions.',
sections:[['Extract consistently','For each paper, record the question, setting, sample or materials, design, findings, and limitations. Keep quotations clearly marked and record page numbers when needed. Distinguish the authors’ claims from your own interpretation.'],['Compare across studies','Group findings by themes or questions. Ask whether apparent disagreements reflect different populations, measures, contexts, or methods. A small interview study and a large survey may address different aspects of the same issue.'],['Evaluate the fit','Assess whether the methods support the claims and whether the evidence applies to your question. Peer review is useful context, but it does not remove the need for critical reading. A relevant source can still have important limitations.']],
before:'Paper A says this. Paper B says that. Paper C says something else.',after:'The studies address competing work and study demands, but differ in how they define the population and collect evidence. Those differences need to be examined before treating their findings as directly comparable.',example:'Use this sentence structure with actual studies and citations. It illustrates synthesis; it is not a summary of a real set of papers.',trap:'Counting how many papers support an idea without checking the quality, independence, and relevance of their evidence.',steps:['Complete a matrix for at least three relevant sources.','Group them by a shared theme or question.','Write a comparison that addresses methods and limitations.'],prompt:'Write one synthesis paragraph using sources you have read. Identify a shared theme, an agreement or difference, and a limitation. Include source identifiers.',placeholder:'The studies address…\nThey agree / differ on…\nA possible methodological explanation is…\nThe evidence is limited by…\nSources…',question:'Which approach best demonstrates synthesis?',answers:['Listing every paper in publication order','Comparing findings and methods across sources around a shared question','Copying each paper’s abstract into a table'],correct:1,feedback:'Synthesis explains relationships between studies rather than simply placing summaries side by side.',checks:['My paragraph compares more than one source.','Methods and limitations are considered.','Source claims and my interpretations are distinct.']},
{phase:1,time:10,output:'An evidence-supported gap',title:'Find a gap you can justify',subtitle:'Explain what remains uncertain without claiming more than you know.',
sections:[['Look for consequential uncertainty','A useful gap may involve conflicting explanations, an underexamined context, incomplete evidence, or a limitation in existing methods. A new location alone does not establish that another study is valuable; explain what the context could reveal.'],['Check the claim','Trace your gap to studies you have read. Ask whether recent publications address it and whether you are missing terminology from another discipline. “No research exists” is a much stronger claim than “the sources reviewed here do not explain…”'],['Connect gap and contribution','State what is known, what remains unclear, and what a feasible study could add. Keep the contribution proportionate to your design. Exploring experiences in one setting does not necessarily produce a general explanation for all students.']],
before:'Nobody has studied this topic before.',after:'The literature reviewed for this proposal does not yet explain how the transition into university shapes work–study decisions in this particular setting. A focused study could explore that contextual question.',example:'This statement would require a documented review and a clear reason why the setting matters. Do not copy it as an established gap.',trap:'Mistaking an unsuccessful search for proof of absence. Revisit terms, databases, and recent work before making a gap claim.',steps:['Write what existing evidence already explains.','Identify a remaining uncertainty with citations.','Explain why resolving it would be useful and feasible.'],prompt:'Write a gap statement using “what is known → what remains unclear → what this study could add.” Name the evidence supporting it.',placeholder:'Existing studies show… [sources]\nWhat remains unclear is…\nThis matters because…\nMy study could contribute…',question:'What is the strongest basis for a research gap?',answers:['The topic is new to me','I could not find anything with one search','A documented uncertainty supported by a critical review'],correct:2,feedback:'A gap is an argument grounded in evidence, not just a claim of novelty.',checks:['The gap refers to specific evidence.','Its importance is explained.','The proposed contribution is realistic.']},
{phase:1,time:10,output:'Aligned research questions',title:'Ask a question you can answer',subtitle:'Make your research direction specific, open, and feasible.',
sections:[['Match the question to the gap','Your main question should address the uncertainty you identified. Supporting questions should unpack that same purpose rather than introduce unrelated topics. Check that the wording fits the people, context, and evidence you intend to study.'],['Choose words carefully','“How do participants describe…” asks about accounts or experiences. “What is the association…” asks about a relationship. “What causes…” requires a design that can support causal inference. These are different research commitments.'],['Check feasibility','Ask what evidence would answer the question, whether you can obtain it ethically, and whether the analysis is manageable. Remove terms that cannot be defined or explored with your proposed methods.']],
before:'Does working ruin the lives of all university students?',after:'How do first-year students with paid jobs at Institution X describe managing work and study demands?',example:'The revised question avoids loaded language, narrows the population, and fits a study of participants’ experiences.',trap:'Packing several studies into one question. If you cannot explain a realistic route to an answer, narrow the scope.',steps:['Draft one main research question.','Add supporting questions only when necessary.','Name the evidence required to answer each one.'],prompt:'Write your main question and up to three supporting questions. For each, state what evidence would answer it.',placeholder:'Main research question…\nEvidence needed…\nSupporting question 1…\nSupporting question 2…\nFeasibility check…',question:'Which question implies a causal claim?',answers:['How do students describe their experience?','What is the association between work hours and reported stress?','Does increasing work time cause academic performance to decline?'],correct:2,feedback:'“Cause” requires stronger design assumptions and evidence than a descriptive or associational question.',checks:['The question addresses the gap.','It avoids loaded or leading wording.','The required evidence is feasible to obtain.']},
{phase:2,time:8,output:'Clear research objectives',title:'Turn questions into objectives',subtitle:'Describe what your study will accomplish.',
sections:[['State the intended knowledge outcome','Objectives describe the purpose of the research, not only the tasks you will perform. “Conduct interviews” is an activity. “Explore how participants manage competing demands” explains what the interviews are intended to help you understand.'],['Align every objective','Pair each objective with a research question and a source of evidence. If you cannot make that connection, either the objective is outside your scope or the question needs revision.'],['Use precise verbs','Describe, explore, compare, estimate, and examine signal different purposes. Select a verb that fits your design. Avoid promising to prove, solve, or eliminate a complex problem through a small study.']],
before:'To collect data and finish the research.',after:'To explore how first-year students with paid jobs describe the strategies they use to manage work and study demands.',example:'This objective describes the intended understanding and aligns with a question about experiences.',trap:'Adding objectives because they sound impressive. Every objective creates a real evidence and analysis obligation.',steps:['Pair each question with one objective.','Use a verb that fits the intended evidence.','Remove objectives your design cannot address.'],prompt:'Create a question → objective → evidence map. Include only objectives your study can realistically address.',placeholder:'Question 1…\nObjective…\nEvidence and analysis…\n\nQuestion 2…\nObjective…\nEvidence and analysis…',question:'Which is a research objective rather than only an activity?',answers:['Send 20 invitations','Explore participants’ approaches to balancing competing demands','Create a spreadsheet'],correct:1,feedback:'The objective states the understanding the study seeks. Invitations and spreadsheets may be supporting activities.',checks:['Each objective maps to a question.','Verbs fit the study design.','Objectives describe knowledge outcomes.']},
{phase:2,time:15,output:'A feasible methods plan',title:'Design a credible way to find out',subtitle:'Connect your question, evidence, analysis, and ethical responsibilities.',
sections:[['Start with the question','Choose a design because it can answer your question. Interviews can explore accounts of experience; surveys can describe measured patterns; other questions may require observation, documents, experiments, or mixed methods. No single method is best for every question.'],['Explain the full chain','Specify the population or materials, sampling approach, recruitment or access, collection process, and analysis. Explain how each decision supports the question. Sample size needs a rationale appropriate to the design; there is no universal number that makes a study rigorous.'],['Plan for constraints and ethics','Consider informed consent, voluntary participation, privacy, secure storage, and the power relationships involved. Check your institution’s review requirements before collecting data. Describe limitations honestly, including selection effects and the boundaries of your claims.']],
before:'I will use interviews because they are easy.',after:'Semi-structured interviews could support a question about how students describe competing demands. The plan must justify recruitment, interview topics, analysis, consent, and how the sample shapes the interpretation.',example:'This is a possible alignment, not a required design. Discuss the choice with a supervisor and follow local ethics procedures.',trap:'Naming a method without explaining analysis. Knowing how data will be interpreted is part of planning how to collect it.',steps:['Map the question to a design and data source.','Describe collection, sampling, and analysis.','Review access, consent, storage, and institutional approvals.'],prompt:'Draft your methods plan: design, participants or materials, access, sampling, collection, analysis, ethics, and limitations.',placeholder:'Design and rationale…\nParticipants / materials and access…\nSampling…\nCollection procedure…\nAnalysis approach…\nConsent, privacy, and approvals…\nLimitations and feasibility…',question:'What should guide your choice of method?',answers:['The method your friends use','The question and the evidence needed to answer it','The method with the most complicated name'],correct:1,feedback:'A credible design connects the question to suitable evidence and an appropriate analysis.',checks:['Design and analysis align with the question.','Sampling and access are explained.','Ethics and limitations are addressed.']},
{phase:2,time:15,output:'A coherent proposal draft',title:'Bring your proposal together',subtitle:'Make the argument for a worthwhile and workable study.',
sections:[['Build one line of reasoning','A proposal explains why a question matters and how it can be investigated. The problem, literature, gap, questions, objectives, and methods should support the same purpose. Revisit earlier sections when a later decision changes the direction.'],['Write for a reviewer','Define key terms, support factual claims, and explain decisions rather than only naming them. Distinguish plans from completed work. Do not invent findings for a study you have not conducted.'],['Review for readiness','Use your institution’s required structure and citation style. Check references against the original sources, identify practical dependencies, and make the timeline realistic. Ask a supervisor or peer to challenge the connections and assumptions in the draft.']],
before:'A collection of separate sections with different populations and aims.',after:'A connected proposal in which the problem leads to a gap, the gap leads to a question, and the methods can answer that question within the stated scope.',example:'Read your question, objectives, and methods next to one another. Any change in the population, phenomenon, or purpose needs to be explained or corrected.',trap:'Treating a completed checklist as approval. Readiness is a self-review; your institution and supervisor set the requirements.',steps:['Assemble the drafts from each milestone.','Check alignment, sources, ethics, and feasibility.','Record the questions you want a reviewer to address.'],prompt:'Write your proposal summary and reviewer questions. State the problem, question, intended contribution, method, and key limitations.',placeholder:'Working title…\nProblem and gap…\nMain question and objective…\nProposed method…\nExpected contribution (not findings)…\nLimitations…\nQuestions for my reviewer…',question:'Which statement belongs in a proposal for a study not yet conducted?',answers:['Invented findings that support the hypothesis','A justified plan for collection and analysis','A guarantee that the study will solve the problem'],correct:1,feedback:'A proposal presents a justified plan. It should not fabricate results or guarantee an outcome.',checks:['The sections form a connected argument.','Claims and citations have been checked.','The draft follows institutional requirements.']}
];
const phases=[{name:'Find your direction',label:'FOUNDATIONS',desc:'Turn curiosity into a focused problem and a purposeful search.',color:'mint'},{name:'Connect the evidence',label:'DISCOVERY',desc:'Read critically, identify a gap, and ask a researchable question.',color:'blue'},{name:'Build your proposal',label:'CREATION',desc:'Align your objectives, methods, and argument into one plan.',color:'peach'}];
const resources=[
{id:'proposal',name:'Proposal writing workbook',category:'Writing',format:'MD',desc:'A guided outline with section-specific prompts and an alignment review.',use:'When you are ready to assemble your proposal.',preview:['Working title and study context','Evidence-supported problem and gap','Questions, objectives, and framework','Methods, ethics, limitations, and timeline'],content:'# Research proposal workbook\n\nAdapt this structure to your institutional requirements. This workbook guides planning; it is not an approved proposal.\n\n'+course.map((c,i)=>'## '+(i+1)+'. '+stages[i][0]+'\n\n'+c.prompt+'\n\n'+c.placeholder+'\n\nReview:\n'+c.checks.map(x=>'- [ ] '+x).join('\n')).join('\n\n')+'\n\n## References\nList only sources you have read and verified, using your required citation style.\n\n## Timeline and resources\nActivity | Dependency | Time required | Resources\n\n## Reviewer questions\nWhat assumptions or connections should a supervisor check?\n'},
{id:'matrix',name:'Literature review matrix',category:'Evidence',format:'CSV',desc:'Compare questions, methods, findings, and limitations across studies.',use:'As you read each source, before drafting your synthesis.',preview:['Citation and source link','Question, population, and method','Findings and limitations','Theme and relevance to your question'],content:'Citation,DOI or URL,Question,Population or materials,Context,Design,Collection and analysis,Findings,Limitations,Theme,Relevance,Page references,Your interpretation\r\n'},
{id:'search',name:'Literature search log',category:'Evidence',format:'CSV',desc:'Keep your searches traceable and make the next session easier.',use:'Every time you run or refine a literature search.',preview:['Database, date, and exact query','Filters and result count','Screening decisions','Next query or follow-up'],content:'Date,Database,Exact search query,Filters,Results count,Inclusion criteria,Exclusion criteria,Useful sources,Next action\r\n'},
{id:'framework',name:'Conceptual framework guide',category:'Planning',format:'MD',desc:'Define your concepts and explain the relationships you intend to explore.',use:'After reviewing the literature and before finalizing methods.',preview:['Define the central concepts','Explain proposed relationships','Support connections with literature','State assumptions and boundaries'],content:'# Conceptual framework guide\n\n## 1. Anchor the framework\nWrite the research question and the phenomenon you want to understand.\n\n## 2. Define concepts\nFor each concept, write a definition and its scholarly source. Explain any differences in how studies use it.\n\n## 3. Map relationships\nDescribe each proposed link and the evidence or theory behind it. A diagram does not demonstrate causation.\n\n## 4. Explain boundaries\nState your assumptions, context, and alternative explanations. Distinguish your own conceptual framework from an established theory.\n\n## 5. Connect to methods\nExplain how the framework informs what you collect and how you interpret it.\n\nReview: Can you explain every connection? Are definitions consistent? Have you cited the original sources?\n'},
{id:'methods',name:'Methods planning worksheet',category:'Planning',format:'MD',desc:'Work through design, sampling, analysis, and ethical considerations.',use:'Once your main research question is sufficiently focused.',preview:['Question → evidence → design','Participants, materials, and access','Collection and analysis plan','Consent, privacy, feasibility, and limits'],content:'# Methods planning worksheet\n\n'+['Research question: What exactly needs answering?','Evidence: What observations or materials could answer it?','Design: Why does this approach fit?','Sampling: Who or what will be included, and why?','Access: What permissions or resources are needed?','Collection: What will happen, in what order?','Analysis: How will the evidence be interpreted?','Ethics: What consent, review, privacy, and storage arrangements are required?','Feasibility: What are the dependencies and resource limits?','Limitations: What claims will this design not support?'].map(s=>'## '+s+'\n\nYour plan:\n').join('\n')},
{id:'checklist',name:'Proposal readiness checklist',category:'Writing',format:'MD',desc:'Check coherence and practicality before sending a draft for feedback.',use:'Before a supervisor meeting or proposal submission.',preview:['Problem and evidence','Question–objective–method alignment','Ethics and feasibility','Sources and institutional requirements'],content:'# Proposal readiness checklist\n\nThis is a self-review, not institutional approval.\n\n'+course.flatMap(c=>c.checks).map(x=>'- [ ] '+x).join('\n')+'\n\n## Reviewer feedback\nStrengths:\nQuestions:\nPriority revisions:\nNext review date:\n'}
];
