 "model": {
  "title": "A source-linked plan, from beginning to end",
  "steps": [
   [
    "Question",
    "Ask how first-year students at one campus describe managing study communication and participation. The example investigates experience; it does not claim an intervention improves grades."
   ],
   [
    "Search",
    "Plan two concept groups: undergraduate / university student and study communication / participation. Try subject databases, screen relevance and record actual dates and filters. A planned query is not a completed review."
   ],
   [
    "Read",
    "Use Fuchs for communication and privacy accounts, Sarkar and colleagues for mixed-method remote-learning experiences, and Boulton and colleagues for engagement across a term. Their populations and designs differ."
   ],
   [
    "Synthesize",
    "Communication, participation and context are useful sensitizing ideas. These papers do not directly answer the proposed local question, and this small teaching set does not prove a global research gap."
   ],
   [
    "Design",
    "Propose interviews with a justified selection of first-year students after access and ethics review. Use concrete recent situations as prompts, develop an explicit qualitative analysis and examine contrasting accounts."
   ],
   [
    "Write and review",
    "State what is proposed, what published sources actually show and what remains unknown. There are no collected local results. Check source passages, methods and limits with a supervisor before using the plan."
   ]
  ],
  "proposal": "# Worked example: a proposed local study\n\nTeaching model. The cited publications are real. The local study below is illustrative, has not been conducted, and reports no local findings.\n\n## Question and rationale\nHow do first-year students at one campus describe managing study communication and participation? The practical motivation is to understand situated experiences before proposing local support. The three example sources are a teaching set, not an exhaustive review or evidence of worldwide novelty.\n\n## Published evidence and its boundaries\nFuchs (2022), DOI 10.3389/feduc.2022.834391, reports interviews with undergraduates in Thailand, including privacy and communication themes. Sarkar et al. (2022), DOI 10.3389/fmed.2022.834228, combines survey and interview evidence about remote health-professions education at one Australian institution. Boulton et al. (2019), DOI 10.1371/journal.pone.0225770, follows engagement and wellbeing across a term at a UK university. These sources motivate questions about communication and participation. They study different populations, measures and contexts and do not establish what students at our campus experience.\n\n## Proposed methods\nAfter supervisor and institutional review, seek voluntary participation from first-year students with relevant study experiences. Justify selection for useful variation rather than claiming statistical representation. Develop semi-structured prompts around recent communication and participation situations; pilot their clarity. Plan consent, withdrawal, confidentiality, de-identification, secure retention and an appropriate relationship between researcher and participants.\n\nRead and compare accounts, record initial codes, develop and review themes, examine contrasting cases, and keep a reflexive decision record. Explain how interpretations connect to excerpts. Confirm the specific analytic approach and adequacy of the evidence with the supervisor.\n\n## Intended contribution and limits\nThe intended output is a bounded account of experience. Interviews alone would not estimate campus-wide prevalence, demonstrate grade improvement or isolate an intervention effect. Access, selection, interview language, researcher interpretation and setting must be considered. No results should be written until evidence is collected and analysed.\n\n## Next decision\nReview the literature coverage and institutional requirements, then decide whether the proposed question is useful and feasible in the actual local setting.\n"
 }
